          "metadata": {}
        },
        "create_time": 1774602938.788468,
        "update_time": null,
        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "In a condo board **AGM (Annual General Meeting)**, a **motion** is simply a **formal proposal** that members are asked to consider and vote on.\n\n### Think of it like this:\nA motion = *“I suggest we do this — do we all agree?”*\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 Example of a motion\n- “I move that the condo fees increase by 5%.”\n- “I move that we approve the new security contract.”\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 How a motion works step-by-step\n1. **Someone proposes it** (called “moving” the motion)  \n2. **Another person seconds it** (shows at least two people think it’s worth discussing)  \n3. **Discussion happens** (owners can speak for or against it)  \n4. **Vote is taken**  \n5. **Result** → Passed or defeated  \n\n---\n\n### 🔹 Why motions matter\nMotions are how **decisions become official** at the AGM. Without a motion, nothing can be formally approved.\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 Types of motions you might see\n- Approving budget or financial statements  \n- Electing board members  \n- Changing bylaws or rules  \n- Hiring management or security companies  \n\n---\n\nIf you want, I can also show you **how to write a strong motion** for your own issue (like complaints or policy changes in your condo)."
          ]
        },
